WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:07.820
You've now learned about several highly effective neural network and confnet architectures.

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:12.140
What I want to do in the next few videos is share with you some practical advice on how

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:17.060
to use them, first starting with using open source implementations.

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:22.820
It turns out that a lot of these neural networks are difficult or finicky to replicate because

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:28.700
a lot of details about tuning the hyperparameters such as learning rate decay and other things

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:31.540
that make some difference to the performance.

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:38.180
And so I found that it's sometimes difficult even for say AI or deep learning PhD students

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:43.620
even at the top universities to replicate someone else's published work just from reading the

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:44.900
research paper.

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:51.260
Fortunately, a lot of deep learning researchers routinely open source their work on the internet

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:52.940
such as on GitHub.

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:58.260
And as you do work yourself, I certainly encourage you to consider contributing back your code

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:00.500
to the open source community.

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:05.220
But if you see a research paper whose results you would like to build on top of, one thing

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:10.080
you should consider doing, one thing I do quite often is just look online for an open

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:12.120
source implementation.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:16.940
Because if you can get the author's implementation, you can usually get going much faster than

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:20.140
you would try to re implement it from scratch.

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:25.100
Although certainly re implementing from scratch could be a good exercise to do as well.

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:30.460
If you're already familiar with how to use GitHub, this video might be less necessary

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:32.180
or less important for you.

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:36.600
But if you aren't used to downloading open source code from GitHub, let me quickly show

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:40.780
you how easy it is.

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:45.420
Let's say you've heard about, let's say you're excited about residual networks and you

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:46.420
want to use it.

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:51.060
So let's search for resnets on GitHub.

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:55.460
And so you actually see a lot of different implementations of resnets on GitHub.

00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:59.060
I'm just going to go to the first URL here.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:02.220
And this is a GitHub repo that implements resnets.

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:07.980
A lot of the GitHub web pages you scroll down will have some text describing the work of

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:10.140
the particular implementation.

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:15.860
This particular repo, this particular GitHub repo repository was actually by the original

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:19.260
authors of the resnet paper.

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:25.260
And this code, this license under MIT license, you can click through to take a look at

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:27.140
the implications of this license.

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:32.500
The MIT license is one of the more permissive or one of the more open open source licenses.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:35.620
So I'm going to go ahead and download the code.

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:37.740
And to do that, click on this link.

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:41.020
This gives you the URL that you can use to download the code.

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:45.860
I'm going to click this button over here to copy the URL to my clipboard and then go

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:46.860
over here.

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:53.200
And all you have to do is type git clone and then control V for the URL and hit enter.

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:58.240
And so in a couple seconds, it has downloaded, it has cloned this repository to my local hard

00:02:58.240 --> 00:02:59.200
disk.

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:01.920
So let's go into the directory.

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:02.920
Let's take a look.

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:07.360
I'm more used to Mac than Windows, but I guess let's see.

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:09.760
Let's go to Prototex.

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:15.520
And I think this is where it has the files specifying the network.

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:21.320
So let's take a look at this file because this is a very long file that specifies the

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:28.080
detailed configurations of the ResNet with 101 layers.

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:34.400
And it looks like from what I remember seeing from this web page, this particular implementation

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:39.650
uses the CAFE framework.

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:44.170
But if you want an implementation of this code using some other programming framework,

00:03:44.170 --> 00:03:48.560
you might be able to find it as well.

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:54.160
So if you're developing a computer vision application, a very common workflow would be

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:59.320
to pick an architecture that you like, maybe one of the ones you learn about in this course,

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:03.800
or maybe one that you heard about from a friend or from some of the literature, and look for

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:09.680
an open source implementation and download it from GitHub to start building from there.

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:14.480
One of the advantages of doing so also is that sometimes these networks take a long

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:19.640
time to train and someone else might have used multiple GPUs and a very large dataset

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:22.180
to pre-train some of these networks.

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:26.900
And that allows you to do transfer learning using these networks, which we'll discuss

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:28.940
in the next video as well.

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:34.300
Of course, if you're a computer vision researcher implementing these things from scratch, then

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:36.380
your workflow would be different.

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:40.860
And if you do that, then do contribute your work back to the open source community.

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:46.660
But because so many vision researchers have done so much work implementing these architectures,

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:52.220
I've found that often starting with open source implementations is a better way or is

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:55.780
certainly a faster way to get started on the new project.
